    {
      "role": "user",
      "content": "That makes sense. Regarding the technical side, what kind of specific security risks or \"threat actors\" should we be most worried about when transitioning to hardware keys? Like, what specifically makes them better against the stuff that usually bypasses SMS?",
      "source": "evaluator"
    },
    {
      "role": "assistant",
      "content": "Hardware keys are superior because they eliminate SIM-swapping and SMS interception attacks entirely by using cryptographic challenges that cannot be intercepted over the air. Unlike SMS, which is vulnerable to social engineering and network delays, FIDO2 keys perform authenticator attestation, proving to the server that the physical device is actually in possession of the user, making remote replay attacks impossible. While you must remain vigilant against corporate espionage attempts to steal these devices physically, the underlying protocol protects the password from being sent across your internal network even if a hacker has admin-level access to your servers.",
